---
// The grid behind every page. Purely decorative, so it is aria-hidden and
// pointer-events:none. Blueprint carries no ambient wash at all — see below.
//
// Deliberately ONE fixed layer with an opaque base. The earlier version
// stacked three fixed boxes running
// `filter: blur(90px)` on a permanent 22s animation, plus a second masked
// fixed layer — five-plus continuously-invalidated composited layers. That
// combination produced stale-paint artifacts on real hardware: the page text
// composited over leftover pixels from a previously viewed page.
//
// Radial gradients are already soft, so the blur filter bought nothing that
// colour stops cannot, and dropping the animation removes the compositor's
// reason to re-rasterise a full-viewport blurred layer on every frame.
---

<div class="bg" aria-hidden="true"></div>

<style>
  .bg {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* Opaque base. Belt-and-braces with body's background: if the compositor
       ever drops one paint, the other still covers the viewport. */
    /* Flat. The radial washes that every dark theme carried are gone: on a
       light ground a coloured glow reads as a printing defect rather than
       atmosphere, and Swiss layout gets its depth from rules, not light. */
    background-color: var(--bg);
  }

  /* The grid rides on the same layer as a pseudo-element rather than a second
     fixed box, so nothing here can promote an extra composited layer.
     Unmasked now — a blueprint grid that fades out in the corners is a glow
     effect wearing a grid's clothes. It runs edge to edge at low contrast
     instead, which is what makes the page read as drafting paper. */
  .bg::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    /* --line is 0.18 dark-on-light, so the effective grid contrast is ~0.08.
       Strong enough to read as structure, weak enough to sit under text. */
    opacity: 0.45;
    background-image:
      linear-gradient(var(--line) 1px, transparent 1px),
      linear-gradient(90deg, var(--line) 1px, transparent 1px);
    background-size: 32px 32px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .bg::after { background-size: 24px 24px; }
  }
</style>
